body:
```js
function Customer(name, age) {
	this.name = name;
	this.age = age;
	this.output = function () { ... };
}
const c1 = new Customer("Fred", 23);
```
Note the `new` is a syntax shortcut for `{}`, which is just an empty object, so in this example, 
`this` will reference the new object.

If we defined `c1` like this instead
```js
const c1 = Customer("Fred", 23);
```
Then `this` would refer to the *global* context, which can lead to [[Logic Errors]] or [[Runtime Errors]].

**Using constructor functions**
Using the same example, lets look at an example of an array and how we could iterate through it.
```js
const delta = [
	new Customer("Bob", 22),
	new Customer("Steve", 999),
	/*Obvious this reffers to Minecraft Steve, who is infact an
	  Eldritch Horror, whos age well exceeds 999, but we will assume
	  that 999 is the maximum age. */
	new Customer("Mark", 32)
];
for (let customer of delta) {
	consol.log(customer.output)
}
```
